.\" Man page for the send_reminders Django management command
.\" scd-send-reminders(1)
.TH SCD\-SEND\-REMINDERS 1 "2026-09-08" "SCD Effort Reporting" "SCD Effort Reporting Commands"
.SH NAME
scd\-send\-reminders \- fire due activity-report reminder schedules
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B python manage.py send_reminders
[\fB\-\-list\fR]
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
[\fB\-\-schedule\fR \fIID\fR]
[\fB\-\-force\fR]
[\fB\-\-recipient\fR \fIEMAIL\fR]
[\fB\-\-template\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-\-ignore\-interval\fR]
[\fB\-\-now\fR \fIISO8601\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B send_reminders
evaluates every enabled reminder schedule, and for each one whose next run time
has passed, mails an activity-report reminder to the people in the schedule
owner's scope.
.PP
The command is the supported production driver for scheduled reminders. Run it
from an OKD CronJob (see
.IR helm/simple/templates/reminder\-cronjob.yaml )
or from system cron, often enough that a schedule is noticed shortly after it
comes due. It does not need to run at the moment a reminder should go out: the
wall-clock rule ("Friday at 15:00 America/Chicago") is stored on the schedule
itself, so a tick every 15 minutes delivers each reminder at most 15 minutes
late.
.PP
Running it concurrently is safe. Each due schedule is claimed with a single
atomic conditional UPDATE, so when several web workers, several cron ticks, or a
cron tick and the optional in-process ticker all decide the same schedule is
due, exactly one of them sends it. The claim is taken before any mail goes out,
so a crash mid-batch skips that firing rather than replaying it \(em a missed
nudge is much cheaper than a duplicated one.
.PP
Recipients are resolved from the schedule owner's role every time the schedule
fires, never frozen at creation time: a group leader reaches their own group, a
division head the groups they manage, a functional lead the authors of entries
booked against the projects they lead, and an administrator everyone. Somebody
who joins a group after the schedule was created is picked up on the next
firing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print every schedule with its owner, template, cadence, and next and last run
times, then exit. Schedules that are currently due are marked
.BR DUE .
Sends nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report what would be sent without sending mail, writing to the reminder log, or
advancing any schedule's next run time. Combine with
.B \-\-schedule
to rehearse one schedule.
.TP
.BI \-\-schedule " ID"
Act on one schedule only, by the numeric id shown in
.BR \-\-list .
Without
.BR \-\-force ,
a schedule that is not yet due is reported and skipped.
.TP
.B \-\-force
With
.BR \-\-schedule ,
run the schedule even though it is not due. Its next run time is still advanced
from now, so a forced run does not cause a second delivery at the original slot.
.TP
.BI \-\-recipient " EMAIL"
Send a single reminder to one address and ignore schedules entirely. Useful for
verifying SMTP and the rendered body against a real mailbox.
.TP
.BI \-\-template " NAME"
Template to use with
.BR \-\-recipient ,
by the name shown on the Reminders \(-> Email body page. Defaults to the
template marked as default.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-interval
With
.BR \-\-recipient ,
bypass the minimum re-send interval
.RB ( REMINDER_MIN_INTERVAL_HOURS )
so a test reminder is not skipped as a duplicate.
.TP
.BI \-\-now " ISO8601"
Treat the given instant as the current time. Intended for testing and for
catch-up runs after an outage. A naive value is interpreted in the site
timezone.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings resolve in the project-wide order \(em command line, then environment,
then
.IR .env ,
then
.IR config/reminders.yaml ,
then built-in defaults. See
.I config/reminders.yaml.example
for the annotated file.
.TP
.B REMINDER_BASE_URL
Absolute site root used to build the links inside the mail. Required for
scheduled sends, which have no HTTP request to derive it from; falls back to
.RI https:// $SCD_HOSTNAME
when that is set. Without it, a scheduled reminder carries relative links and
is useless to the recipient.
.TP
.B REMINDER_MIN_INTERVAL_HOURS
A recipient reminded within this many hours is skipped. Default 20. Set to 0 to
disable the guard.
.TP
.B REMINDER_STALE_DAYS
Days without an entry before somebody counts as overdue. Default 14. Used as
the default on the recipients page and for new schedules; each schedule stores
its own threshold.
.TP
.B REMINDER_BATCH_SIZE
Upper bound on the recipients one send will process. Default 200.
.TP
.B REMINDER_SCHEDULER_ENABLED
Set to 1 to run the scheduler as a daemon thread inside the web process instead
of driving it from cron. Off by default: it is safe with several gunicorn
workers, but it ties reminder delivery to web-process uptime.
.TP
.B REMINDER_SCHEDULER_INTERVAL
Seconds between ticks of that in-process scheduler. Default 60, clamped to a
minimum of 15.
.PP
SMTP itself is configured by
.BR EMAIL_HOST ,
.BR EMAIL_PORT ,
.BR EMAIL_HOST_USER ,
.BR EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD ,
and
.BR DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL .
With
.B EMAIL_HOST
unset, Django's console backend is used and reminders are printed rather than
delivered \(em which is what the development settings do unconditionally.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 when the run completed, even if individual deliveries failed; per
recipient failures are recorded in the reminder log and summarised on stdout.
Returns non-zero for a usage error, such as an unknown schedule id, an unknown
recipient address, or an unparsable
.BR \-\-now .
.SH EXAMPLES
Show every schedule and which are due:
.PP
.RS
.nf
python manage.py send_reminders \-\-list
.fi
.RE
.PP
Rehearse the whole run without sending anything:
.PP
.RS
.nf
python manage.py send_reminders \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Normal cron invocation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
*/15 * * * * cd /app && python manage.py send_reminders >> logs/reminders.log 2>&1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Send one schedule now, before its slot:
.PP
.RS
.nf
python manage.py send_reminders \-\-schedule 3 \-\-force
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check SMTP and the rendered body against your own mailbox:
.PP
.RS
.nf
python manage.py send_reminders \-\-recipient anorman@fnal.gov \-\-ignore\-interval
.fi
.RE
.PP
Same, inside the running pod:
.PP
.RS
.nf
oc \-n scd\-reporting exec deploy/web \-\- \\
    python manage.py send_reminders \-\-list
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config/reminders.yaml
Operational settings; see
.I config/reminders.yaml.example
for the annotated template.
.TP
.I apps/reminders/scheduling.py
Firing-time arithmetic and the atomic claim.
.TP
.I apps/reminders/service.py
Rendering, the dedupe window, and delivery.
.TP
.I logs/scd_reporting.log
Where send attempts and SMTP errors are logged.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scd-vault-check (1),
.BR start-scd-reporting (1),
.BR restart-pod (1)
.PP
Reminders are managed from the web UI at
.IR /reminders/ :
recipients and ad-hoc sends at the top level,
.I /reminders/templates/
for the editable body,
.I /reminders/schedules/
for recurring sends, and
.I /reminders/log/
for the delivery history.
